# RCX8

An emulator, disassembler and small C library for the RCx8, a fictional 8-bit
microcontroller from a programming puzzle.

## Overview

The RCx8 has:

- 8 general-purpose registers `R0`–`R7`, an accumulator (`ACC`), a carry bit and
  a 16-bit program counter
- 64 KiB of flat memory, zeroed on reset
- 256 I/O ports (`WMP` writes `ACC` to a port, `RDR` reads one)
- an internal call stack (`JMS` pushes, `BBL` pops)
- keyboard input (`KBI`) and ASCII output (`ASC`)
- 1–3 byte instructions; addresses are stored LSB first

The full instruction set (encoding, carry behaviour, size, cycles) is documented
in [doc/inst.htm](doc/inst.htm). The original problem statement is in
[doc/prog3_proc.txt](doc/prog3_proc.txt).

## Building

Requires CMake (>= 3.5) and a C compiler.

```sh
mkdir -p build && cd build
cmake ..
make
```

This produces:

| Target                                   | Description                         |
|------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| `rcx8`                                   | emulator (`src/main.c`)             |
| `rcx8_disasm`                            | disassembler (`src/disasm.c`)       |
| `librcx8_shared.so`, `librcx8_static.a`  | CPU core and loader as a library    |

## Usage

```sh
rcx8 [FLAGS] PROGRAM.prg
```

`FLAGS` is a single argument made up of the following letters:

| Flag | Description                                                                 |
|------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `v`  | Verbose. Print loader info and every executed instruction; log to `out.log`. |
| `s`  | Step mode. Pause (press Enter) at `JUN`, `JMS` and `BBL`, and when PC reaches `0x1000` or `0x1100`, printing the CPU state. |

After the program halts in step mode, the emulator executes one instruction per
line read from stdin. Entering `p` prints the CPU state; `d` writes all of
memory to `dump.out`.

Examples:

```sh
./build/rcx8 rcx8.prg         # run a program
./build/rcx8 s rcx8.prg       # step through it
./build/rcx8 v demo_menu.prg  # trace execution
```

`KBI` prints a `kbi: ` prompt before reading a character from stdin.

### Disassembler

```sh
rcx8_disasm [v] PROGRAM.prg
```

This is a linear sweep that prints a fixed number of instructions (868,
hardcoded in `src/disasm.c`) and does not distinguish code from data. Adjust
the loop bound for other programs.

## Program file format (`.prg`)

A `.prg` file is a sequence of little-endian 16-bit words forming blocks:

| Block type | Layout                                                          |
|------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
| `0x0000`   | End of file                                                     |
| `0x1000`   | Comment: `word length`, followed by ASCII bytes (printed on load) |
| `0x2000`   | Data: `word address`, `word length`, followed by data bytes     |
| `0x7000`   | Start address: `word pc`                                        |

## Repository layout

| Path                      | Description                                         |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| `src/program.c`, `.h`     | CPU core: instruction decode, execute, `op2str`     |
| `src/loader.c`, `.h`      | `.prg` loader (`bootstrap`) and memory dump         |
| `src/main.c`              | emulator front end                                  |
| `src/disasm.c`            | disassembler front end                              |
| `doc/`                    | instruction set reference and problem statement     |
| `rcx8.prg`, `rcx8_2.prg`  | puzzle programs                                     |
| `test.prg`                | minimal test program                                |
| `demo_menu.prg`           | demo program: keyboard-driven text menu             |

## Notes

- The puzzle program decrypts its own code around `0x1000`–`0x1200`. The
  emulator keeps running past `0x1200` rather than stopping there.
- The loader reads 16-bit words directly from the file, so it assumes a
  little-endian host.
- ADD/SUB/IAC carry handling and DAA follow the specification in
  `doc/inst.htm`.
- `execute_op` passes the CPU state by value, which is simple but slow.
- The call stack holds 128 entries and wraps on overflow; `BBL` does not check
  for underflow.

## Known issues / TODO

- Verify endianness handling in the loader.
- Make the disassembler follow the actual program length and instruction sizes.
